   "source": [
    "# Simple Engineered Features\n",
    "\n",
    "Why Feature Engineering?\n",
    "\n",
    "Feature engineering creates new variables from existing ones that may better capture relationships in the data than the original variables alone.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Unlike feature selection (which removes features), feature engineering creates new information that may improve model performance while still remaining interpretable.\n",
    "\n",
    "- For this Formula 1 dataset, the engineered features are based on financial and operational relationships that are meaningful in real-world Formula 1 economics."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3e6d0902",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Feature 1\n",
    "\n",
    "Sponsorship Efficiency\n",
    "\n",
    "Formula\n",
    "\n",
    "     Sponsorship Efficiency = Operating Budget/Sponsorship Revenue"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 59,
   "id": "4008f382",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "merged[\"sponsorship_efficiency\"] = (\n",
    "    merged[\"sponsorship_revenue_usd_m\"] /\n",
    "    merged[\"operating_budget_usd_m\"]\n",
    ")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "62e440dc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Feature 2\n",
    "\n",
    "Budget Remaining Under Cost Cap\n",
    "\n",
    "Formula\n",
    "\n",
    "    Budget Margin=Cost Cap−Operating Budget"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
