  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9331668b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Feature engineering was performed by creating domain-specific variables such as Sponsorship Efficiency, Budget Margin, Sponsorship Percentage, and Cost Cap Utilization to better represent the financial characteristics of Formula 1 teams. Feature selection was subsequently conducted using Correlation Analysis, Mutual Information, ANOVA F-Test, and Random Forest Feature Importance. The results consistently identified Operating Budget as the most influential feature, followed by Sponsorship Revenue, selected team indicators (particularly Mercedes and Red Bull Racing), Season, and Cost Cap Limit. Several engineered features provided limited additional predictive value and were therefore not prioritised for the final model. Additionally, Total Revenue Group was identified as a potential source of target leakage and should be retained only if it is verified to be independent of the target variable. Combining multiple feature selection techniques produced a compact, interpretable, and statistically justified feature set suitable for both the regression task (predicting Prize Money) and the classification task (predicting Constructors' Championship). This approach improves model interpretability, reduces unnecessary complexity, and provides a more robust basis for subsequent machine learning modelling."
   ]
